<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dvaita Vedanta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Dvaita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dvaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> is a dualist school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> philosophy founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a> in the 13th century in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. It teaches that God, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, individual souls and matter are eternally distinct, in contrast to the non-dualism of Advaita. Its monasteries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udupi">Udupi</a> remain its main centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dvaita-vedanta.jpg" alt="Dvaita Vedanta" width="320" height="343" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AR767 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shri_Madhvacharya_Vigraha,_Pajaka,_Udupi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
